.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 304 PM PST Mon Nov 16 2026
(This evening through Tuesday)

Our primary frontal band continues to march eastward through the
Central Valley this afternoon. Daytime heating in the wake of the
morning rain has boosted instability. In fact, SPC mesoanalysis
indicates anywhere form 200-500 J/kg of surface based CAPE which
corroborates with afternoon ACARS data from SFO, SJC, and OAK.
Past radar observations have indicated transient rotational
signatures and given some of the photographs received, the
convection we`ve been dealing with is more of a non-mesocyclonic
variety. This type of convection typically acquires its rotation
by tilting and stretching of near surface vorticity upward into
convection (and a majority of this convection is devoid of any
lightning). This is in contract to more classic/mini-supercell
structures which derive their rotation largely from the
environmental deep layer shear. 0-3km MLCAPE values (think of this
as a proxy for an updrafts ability to tilt/stretch near surface
vorticity) and surface vorticity (the actual spin needed for
waterspouts/landspouts) were maximized over SF Bay and along the
Pacific Coast this morning. For the rest of this
afternoon/evening, we`ll continue to be mindful of the potential
for non-mesocyclonic tornadoes (landspouts/waterspouts), but this
threat should wane as we loose daytime heating (0-3km MLCAPE will
decline). However, there will remain a potential for showers and
perhaps a rumble of thunder or two after nightfall, particularly
across the Pacific Coast ahead of the main upper trough.

As the upper trough scoots eastward through after midnight, this
should effectively shut off large scale ascent across the region.
In the wake of this feature, large scale subsidence and clearing
skies should promote ideal radiational cooling. However, the
surface MSLP gradient magnitude may remain sufficiently large such
that the boundary layer doesn`t completely decouple. That said,
it does appear that we`ll get cold across some of the low-lying
and sheltered regions. As such, we`ve issued a Cold Weather
Advisory for the North and East Bay interior valleys, as well as
the southern Salinas Valley tonight into Tuesday morning. If you
lack adequate heating, be sure to check with local, city, or
county officials for warming centers. If using a portable heater,
be sure they are in proper working order and used correctly! Be
sure to cover/tend to sensitive vegetation and/or pets.

