.LONG TERM...
Issued at 346 PM PST Mon Nov 16 2026
(Tuesday night through next Sunday)

Winds are forecast to strengthen ahead of an approaching cold
front Tuesday night. Thus, we have issued a Wind Advisory for the
coastal locations and the East Bay Interior Valley for southwest
winds of 20 to 30 mph with gusts up to 50 from 9 PM Tuesday
through 1 PM Wednesday. For the same time frame, we have a High
Wind Warning for the East Bay Hills where gusts of up to 70 mph
are expected with the strongest wind gusts being around the
Altamont Pass.

Rain will begin to fall ahead of the cold front late Tuesday
evening across the North Bay and then spread southward into the
Bay Area and Santa Cruz Mountains early Wednesday morning. As the
front progresses southward, it will move through the remainder of
the Central Coast late morning and early afternoon. There is also
the potential for isolated thunderstorms as MUCAPE values approach
350 J/kg along the frontal boundary.

Post-frontal rain showers are forecast to persist Wednesday night
and into Thursday morning as a colder air mass advect southward
from the north. This will also lead to increased potential for
thunderstorms with as a surface low pressure system forecast to
move into the Bay Area or Central Coast midday Thursday. This is
when midlevel lapse rates approach 6-8 deg C/km and MUCAPE values
between 300-600 J/kg.

Tuesday night through Friday morning, we can expect 1"-2" of
rainfall in the rain shadowed valleys, 1.5"-2.5" across the rest
of the Bay Area and Central Coast, 2"-3.5" in the North Bay, and
3"-5" in the Santa Cruz Mountains and Santa Lucia Range. Rain
showers and thunderstorm potential will diminish in coverage early
Friday morning as the surface and mid/upper level low push
farther inland. Temperatures are forecast to cool once again both
Friday and Saturday morning into the low-to-mid 30s in the North
and East Bay Valleys as well as the southern Salinas Valley.

Unsettled conditions return to the region for this upcoming
weekend with increasing rain chances. However, we are still to far
out in time to start messaging the about potential impacts.
Remember to keep up-to-date with the latest forecast information!

