.AVIATION...
(00Z TAFS)
Issued at 431 PM PST Mon Nov 16 2026

An absolutely interesting afternoon across the region, as the main
front worked its way through. Behind it, clear skies and a lingering
trough led to an active afternoon of weather. Visually we had a mix
of cumulus clouds across the region, with some high clouds exiting
the region. Showers and isolated thunderstorms rolled through
region. We still have a few showers and cumulus clouds lingering
with conditions slowly improving this evening. As we head into the
overnight hours, the question will if any fog forms tonight. Highest
confidence is in the Sonoma Valleys, though interior Napa Valleys
could see it too. Any fog that does develop should mix out by mid to
late Tuesday morning. VFR conditions then prevail with winds turning
south late in the afternoon/early evening hours.

Vicinity of SFO...Similar to above, we`ll having lingering cumulus
clouds pass through the region this evening with the potential for a
few vicinity or short lived showers near or over SFO. Models aren`t
doing too terribly well with the cigs associated with these
features, especially as the trough is still kind of lingering
overhead. Opted to keep cigs around/near 050 through 6Z, with the
hope that we should see clouds and showers slowly fizzling out after
sunset. Confidence is low on this and we`ll need to keep an eye on
the timing. VFR conditions are expected to prevail late tonight and
into tomorrow. Winds should generally be onshore through tomorrow
afternoon, with southerly winds picking up after 03Z Wed.

SFO Bridge Approach...Similar to SFO.

Monterey Bay Terminals...SHRA in the vicinity of the terminals is
greatest through the 0400-0500Z timeframe. There`s a low chance (10-
20%) risk that SHRA results in IFR visibility, but confidence is too
low to include in the TAFs at this time, however, trends will be
monitored. Gusty and erratic outflow winds may also be hazardous for
airport operations. The main upper trough responsible for SHRA would
move eastward after 0500Z essentially ending the risk for activity.
However, if the trough slows, this risk may persist longer than
currently advertised. We`ll monitor the potential for additional
SHRA Tuesday afternoon with daytime heating. Outside of the low
potential for reduced visibility, VFR is forecast to prevail through
the valid TAF cycle.&&

.MARINE...
(Tonight through next Sunday)
Issued at 431 PM PST Mon Nov 16 2026

Scattered thunderstorms with the potential for waterspouts and
locally gustier winds continue through this evening. Very rough to
high seas continue through early Tuesday morning with significant
wave heights up to 20 ft. Seas subside slightly Tuesday afternoon
before building again by Wednesday with larger swells arriving.
Hazardous marine conditions return midweek with widespread gale
force gusts, moderate to heavy rain, and the potential for
thunderstorms and waterspouts to occur. Localized storm force
gusts are possible Wednesday into Thursday along the Big Sur
coastline.

